<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-more-on-integrability-and-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    More on integrability and continuity
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a Riemann integrable real-valued function defined on a closed interval. Prove that <m> g \circ f </m> is Riemann integrable for any continuous real-valued function <m> g \colon \R \to \R </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the <xref ref="theorem-heine-cantor-theorem" text="title"/> on an appropriate restriction of <m> g </m>.
    </p>
  </hint>
  
  <!-- <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m> f </m> is Riemann integrable, it is bounded, and so there is a positive real number <m> B \gt 0 </m> so that <m> f \of{ x } \leq B </m> for all points <m> a \leq x \leq b </m>. Since <m> g </m> is continuous on the compact interval <m> \clint{ -B }{ B } </m>, the restriction of <m> g </m> to this compact interval is uniformly continuous by the <xref ref="theorem-heine-cantor-theorem" text="title"/>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </solution> -->
</exercise>
